WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.192
A wallet address can change.

2
00:00:02.192 --> 00:00:05.323
But the identity behind it shouldn't have to disappear.

3
00:00:05.323 --> 00:00:10.784
In decentralized systems, proving who controls an address is only the beginning.

4
00:00:10.784 --> 00:00:14.257
The harder question is what happens when that address changes.

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:18.894
Meet KYVERA.

6
00:00:18.894 --> 00:00:26.644
An open-source identity continuity protocol connecting Bitcoin Cash and Nostr through independent cryptographic proofs.

7
00:00:26.644 --> 00:00:28.394
No shared private keys.

8
00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:30.894
No centralized identity provider.

9
00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.415
Each binding is cryptographically verified.

10
00:00:37.415 --> 00:00:43.665
Independent applications can inspect the evidence and determine whether an authorization chain is valid.

11
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.426
When a wallet changes, KYVERA preserves the authorization history.

12
00:00:51.426 --> 00:00:58.597
The old proof remains historically valid, but the old wallet is no longer the currently authorized destination.

13
00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:10.860
KYVERA also supports challenge-response authentication, revocation, and explicit detection of conflicting or incomplete evidence.

14
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:15.584
When the available information is uncertain, sensitive actions are blocked.

15
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.484
Your keys change.

16
00:01:20.484 --> 00:01:22.563
Your identity continues.

17
00:01:22.563 --> 00:01:23.378
KYVERA.

18
00:01:23.378 --> 00:01:24.576
Keys change.

19
00:01:24.576 --> 00:01:26.352
Trust shouldn't disappear.
